1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,533
Where Did Wirecard's $2 Billion Go?

2
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:05,373
June 18, 2020.

3
00:00:05,373 --> 00:00:09,279
A German payments company is supposed
to publish its annual results.

4
00:00:09,759 --> 00:00:14,959
Instead, its auditors say they can't
confirm one point nine billion euros.

5
00:00:14,959 --> 00:00:17,359
About two point one billion dollars.

6
00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:20,647
Cash the company says is sitting in
two banks in the Philippines.

7
00:00:20,647 --> 00:00:23,847
The banks say they never had it.

8
00:00:23,927 --> 00:00:28,557
Two years earlier, this company was worth
about twenty-four billion euros.

9
00:00:28,717 --> 00:00:32,557
It had replaced one of Germany's biggest
banks in the country's top stock

10
00:00:32,557 --> 00:00:33,557
index.

11
00:00:33,277 --> 00:00:36,477
One week after that
announcement, it was bankrupt.

12
00:00:36,477 --> 00:00:38,321
So where did the money go?

13
00:00:38,481 --> 00:00:40,801
We're going to follow it,
account by account.

14
00:00:40,801 --> 00:00:42,561
This is Billions and Bulls.

15
00:00:42,561 --> 00:00:44,481
Let's follow the money.

16
00:00:45,521 --> 00:00:48,348
Part 1 The Company That Replaced a Bank

17
00:00:48,668 --> 00:00:51,948
Wirecard started in 1999, near Munich.

18
00:00:51,948 --> 00:00:54,188
Its job was simple to describe.

19
00:00:54,188 --> 00:00:58,028
When you pay online, somebody has to move
the money from your card to the

20
00:00:58,028 --> 00:00:59,028
shop.

21
00:00:58,754 --> 00:01:01,074
Wirecard did that.

22
00:01:01,074 --> 00:01:03,714
Picture a pair of sneakers
for one hundred euros.

23
00:01:03,714 --> 00:01:05,268
You type in your card number.

24
00:01:05,268 --> 00:01:09,268
In the background, a processor passes the
payment between the shop, your

25
00:01:09,268 --> 00:01:13,348
bank and the card network, checks it for
fraud, and makes sure the shop

26
00:01:13,348 --> 00:01:14,348
gets paid.

27
00:01:14,228 --> 00:01:16,403
For that, it keeps a small fee.

28
00:01:16,563 --> 00:01:20,083
Repeat that millions of times a
day, and you have a business.

29
00:01:20,563 --> 00:01:24,323
Its first big customers, according to the
German magazine Der Spiegel, were

30
00:01:24,323 --> 00:01:26,928
mostly adult websites and online gambling.

31
00:01:27,248 --> 00:01:29,888
Businesses that many banks
didn't want to touch.

32
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:34,688
In 2002, an Austrian consultant named Markus
Braun put money into the struggling

33
00:01:34,688 --> 00:01:37,408
company and became its chief executive.

34
00:01:38,009 --> 00:01:40,729
Wirecard didn't go public the usual way.

35
00:01:40,729 --> 00:01:45,289
In 2005, it merged into a small listed
company that ran telephone advice

36
00:01:45,289 --> 00:01:46,289
hotlines.

37
00:01:46,178 --> 00:01:50,738
A shortcut onto the stock market, with
fewer questions than a normal listing.

38
00:01:51,298 --> 00:01:52,658
Then it grew.

39
00:01:52,658 --> 00:01:53,658
Fast.

40
00:01:53,378 --> 00:01:57,502
It bought companies in Asia, India,
Brazil and the United States.

41
00:01:57,662 --> 00:02:00,622
It got a banking license and
issued its own cards.

42
00:02:00,622 --> 00:02:04,302
On its own accounts, revenue went from
about four hundred eighty million

43
00:02:04,302 --> 00:02:08,766
euros in 2013 to more than
two billion in 2018.

44
00:02:09,006 --> 00:02:11,806
Profit grew almost as fast.

45
00:02:11,966 --> 00:02:14,286
Some of those deals looked expensive.

46
00:02:14,286 --> 00:02:18,606
In 2015, Wirecard paid about three hundred
forty million euros for a group

47
00:02:18,606 --> 00:02:20,395
of payment businesses in India.

48
00:02:20,395 --> 00:02:24,634
A US nonprofit research group later
concluded, from the filings, that at

49
00:02:24,634 --> 00:02:29,752
least one hundred seventy-five million euros
of that never reached the sellers.

50
00:02:30,152 --> 00:02:35,193
In September 2018, Wirecard joined the
DAX, Germany's index of its thirty

51
00:02:35,193 --> 00:02:36,792
biggest listed companies.

52
00:02:36,792 --> 00:02:40,273
It took the place of Commerzbank, one
of the country's largest banks.

53
00:02:40,433 --> 00:02:44,753
A tech company from a Munich suburb, worth
about twenty-four billion euros.

54
00:02:44,753 --> 00:02:48,113
Germany finally had its own fintech star.

55
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:50,033
Braun was the face of it.

56
00:02:50,033 --> 00:02:54,203
He owned about seven percent of the
company, and in September 2018 his shares

57
00:02:54,203 --> 00:02:57,003
were worth more than one
point six billion euros.

58
00:02:57,003 --> 00:03:01,883
In 2017, he told investors that Wirecard used
the latest artificial intelligence

59
00:03:01,883 --> 00:03:03,271
to analyze its data.

60
00:03:03,431 --> 00:03:07,671
According to later reporting, staff were
often stitching customer data together

61
00:03:07,671 --> 00:03:09,031
in spreadsheets.

62
00:03:09,831 --> 00:03:11,671
Here's the thing about payments, though.

63
00:03:11,671 --> 00:03:14,853
A processor keeps only a small
slice of each sale.

64
00:03:14,853 --> 00:03:18,773
To earn billions, you need an
enormous number of payments.

65
00:03:18,773 --> 00:03:21,974
So where were Wirecard's coming from?

66
00:03:22,853 --> 00:03:25,463
Part 2 Partners Nobody Could See

67
00:03:25,623 --> 00:03:30,103
A big part of the answer was a business
most investors never saw up close.

68
00:03:30,103 --> 00:03:33,143
Wirecard called it third-party acquiring.

69
00:03:33,863 --> 00:03:36,513
The idea, in Wirecard's words, was this.

70
00:03:36,513 --> 00:03:40,673
In some countries, Wirecard didn't have
the licenses to process payments

71
00:03:40,673 --> 00:03:41,673
itself.

72
00:03:41,474 --> 00:03:44,193
So it used local partner
companies that did.

73
00:03:44,193 --> 00:03:48,501
The partners handled the payments, and
Wirecard got a share of the fees.

74
00:03:49,062 --> 00:03:50,982
Three partners mattered most.

75
00:03:50,982 --> 00:03:53,141
One in Dubai, called Al Alam.

76
00:03:53,141 --> 00:03:55,221
One in Singapore, called Senjo.

77
00:03:55,221 --> 00:03:57,793
And one in the
Philippines, called PayEasy.

78
00:03:57,953 --> 00:04:02,273
According to reporting by the Financial
Times in 2019, those three partners

79
00:04:02,273 --> 00:04:04,913
brought in about half of
Wirecard's revenue.

80
00:04:04,913 --> 00:04:07,553
And almost all of its profit.

81
00:04:08,113 --> 00:04:09,222
There was a catch.

82
00:04:09,222 --> 00:04:12,822
The money from this business didn't sit
in Wirecard's own bank accounts.

83
00:04:12,822 --> 00:04:17,382
It sat in escrow accounts, held by a
trustee, supposedly as a safety cushion

84
00:04:17,382 --> 00:04:18,766
for the partners' payments.

85
00:04:18,926 --> 00:04:22,846
Wirecard counted that cash on
its balance sheet anyway.

86
00:04:23,167 --> 00:04:24,606
Think of it like this.

87
00:04:24,606 --> 00:04:28,366
You run a shop, and you tell your bank
you made a big profit last year.

88
00:04:28,366 --> 00:04:30,335
The bank asks to see the cash.

89
00:04:30,494 --> 00:04:32,174
You say it isn't in your account.

90
00:04:32,174 --> 00:04:34,494
It's with a friend
overseas, for safekeeping.

91
00:04:34,494 --> 00:04:36,734
Here's a letter from your friend.

92
00:04:37,054 --> 00:04:40,579
For years, Wirecard's auditors
worked a bit like that bank.

93
00:04:40,739 --> 00:04:43,379
Each year, they needed proof
that the cash was real.

94
00:04:43,379 --> 00:04:45,006
And each year, they got letters.

95
00:04:45,006 --> 00:04:49,245
A court in Singapore later found that one of
these trustees, a small corporate

96
00:04:49,245 --> 00:04:53,245
services firm, had sent letters
confirming that it held almost one point

97
00:04:53,245 --> 00:04:55,328
one billion euros for Wirecard.

98
00:04:55,487 --> 00:04:57,327
The letters were false.

99
00:04:57,727 --> 00:05:02,367
By 2019, according to Wirecard, the
escrow cash sat in the Philippines,

100
00:05:02,367 --> 00:05:06,761
in accounts at two of the country's largest
banks, managed by a local trustee.

101
00:05:07,081 --> 00:05:09,161
One point nine billion euros.

102
00:05:09,161 --> 00:05:13,242
That number would become the most famous
hole in German corporate history.

103
00:05:13,242 --> 00:05:17,117
But before anyone looked inside it, the
people who asked questions had to

104
00:05:17,117 --> 00:05:19,677
get past Wirecard first.

105
00:05:20,717 --> 00:05:23,197
Part 3 Shooting the Messenger

106
00:05:23,277 --> 00:05:24,371
The doubts were old.

107
00:05:24,371 --> 00:05:29,091
In 2008, a German shareholder group
publicly questioned Wirecard's balance

108
00:05:29,091 --> 00:05:30,091
sheet.

109
00:05:29,571 --> 00:05:33,891
In 2015, writers at the Financial Times
started asking how the numbers fit

110
00:05:33,891 --> 00:05:34,891
together.

111
00:05:34,478 --> 00:05:39,438
In 2016, a report from a then-unknown group
called Zatarra accused the company

112
00:05:39,438 --> 00:05:41,118
of fraud and money laundering.

113
00:05:41,118 --> 00:05:43,118
The shares fell.

114
00:05:43,358 --> 00:05:45,803
Wirecard's answer was always the same.

115
00:05:45,963 --> 00:05:49,643
The critics were short sellers, people
betting on the price to fall, and

116
00:05:49,643 --> 00:05:51,563
they were lying to make money.

117
00:05:51,563 --> 00:05:55,483
Germany's financial regulator,
BaFin, opened investigations.

118
00:05:55,483 --> 00:05:57,243
Into the critics.

119
00:05:57,753 --> 00:05:59,754
And the pressure went further.

120
00:05:59,754 --> 00:06:03,976
Wirecard hired a former Libyan
intelligence chief to look into its critics.

121
00:06:04,136 --> 00:06:08,216
Researchers at Citizen Lab, at the
University of Toronto, later found that

122
00:06:08,216 --> 00:06:11,976
journalists, short sellers and investors
who questioned Wirecard had been

123
00:06:11,976 --> 00:06:16,217
targeted by a hacking-for-hire group, some
of them almost daily for months.

124
00:06:16,217 --> 00:06:18,777
Wirecard denied any wrongdoing.

125
00:06:19,408 --> 00:06:24,448
Then, in January 2019, the Financial Times
reported that a senior Wirecard

126
00:06:24,448 --> 00:06:28,770
manager in Singapore was suspected of
forging and backdating contracts.

127
00:06:28,929 --> 00:06:32,689
The material came from inside the
company, from a lawyer in Singapore who

128
00:06:32,689 --> 00:06:34,770
would later go public as
the whistleblower.

129
00:06:34,770 --> 00:06:37,730
Wirecard called the story
false and defamatory.

130
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:40,170
The shares dropped sharply anyway.

131
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:42,729
Here's where the story gets strange.

132
00:06:42,729 --> 00:06:47,849
In February 2019, BaFin banned new bets
against Wirecard's shares for two

133
00:06:47,849 --> 00:06:48,849
months.

134
00:06:48,489 --> 00:06:50,526
It said it wasn't taking sides.

135
00:06:50,526 --> 00:06:53,246
In April, BaFin filed a
criminal complaint.

136
00:06:53,246 --> 00:06:54,686
Not against Wirecard.

137
00:06:54,686 --> 00:06:58,818
Against the two Financial Times reporters,
for alleged market manipulation.

138
00:06:58,818 --> 00:07:03,297
That case was dropped in 2020,
with no evidence found.

139
00:07:03,377 --> 00:07:05,131
Investors kept believing.

140
00:07:05,131 --> 00:07:10,170
In 2019, the Japanese investment group
SoftBank put about nine hundred million

141
00:07:10,170 --> 00:07:11,770
euros into Wirecard.

142
00:07:11,770 --> 00:07:15,690
Most banking analysts stayed
positive until early 2020.

143
00:07:16,288 --> 00:07:20,288
To be fair to those investors, it
wasn't a crazy bet on paper.

144
00:07:20,288 --> 00:07:22,545
Online payments really were booming.

145
00:07:22,705 --> 00:07:25,185
The audited accounts said the
profits were real.

146
00:07:25,185 --> 00:07:28,625
And short sellers do make money
when a share price falls.

147
00:07:28,625 --> 00:07:32,225
So their warnings came with a motive, and
many people chose to believe the

148
00:07:32,225 --> 00:07:33,751
company instead.

149
00:07:34,071 --> 00:07:38,471
If you're enjoying the story so far,
subscribing helps the channel a lot.

150
00:07:38,471 --> 00:07:42,989
Because next, an outside auditor
finally got permission to look.

151
00:07:43,869 --> 00:07:46,589
Part 4 Seven Days in June

152
00:07:46,589 --> 00:07:51,469
To settle the doubts, Wirecard hired a
second accounting firm, KPMG, for

153
00:07:51,469 --> 00:07:52,907
a special investigation.

154
00:07:52,907 --> 00:07:57,146
On April 28, 2020, KPMG
published its report.

155
00:07:57,146 --> 00:08:01,386
It said it could not verify the profits
from the partner business for 2016,

156
00:08:01,386 --> 00:08:03,317
2017 and 2018.

157
00:08:03,476 --> 00:08:05,317
It hadn't received enough documents.

158
00:08:05,317 --> 00:08:09,156
The shares fell twenty-six
percent in a day.

159
00:08:09,237 --> 00:08:13,652
Wirecard's regular auditor, EY, now did
something it hadn't done before.

160
00:08:13,812 --> 00:08:16,932
It went to the banks in the
Philippines and asked them directly.

161
00:08:16,932 --> 00:08:20,292
On June 18, 2020, the answer arrived.

162
00:08:20,292 --> 00:08:23,835
The one point nine billion euros
could not be confirmed.

163
00:08:23,995 --> 00:08:26,955
The company said someone had tried
to deceive its auditors.

164
00:08:26,955 --> 00:08:30,795
The shares lost more than
sixty percent that day.

165
00:08:30,875 --> 00:08:34,207
The two banks were BDO and the Bank
of the Philippine Islands.

166
00:08:34,447 --> 00:08:38,047
Both said Wirecard was not their client,
and that the documents carrying

167
00:08:38,047 --> 00:08:39,487
their names were fake.

168
00:08:39,487 --> 00:08:41,727
The Philippine central bank went further.

169
00:08:41,727 --> 00:08:45,516
It said none of the money had ever entered
the country's financial system.

170
00:08:45,675 --> 00:08:50,155
One bank suspended an employee whose
signature appeared on one of the documents.

171
00:08:50,155 --> 00:08:54,795
The other said it believed one of its own
officers had faked a bank certificate.

172
00:08:55,319 --> 00:08:57,079
Now the week moves fast.

173
00:08:57,079 --> 00:08:59,239
June 19, Braun resigns.

174
00:08:59,239 --> 00:09:04,199
June 22, Wirecard says the money most
likely doesn't exist, and Braun is

175
00:09:04,199 --> 00:09:05,199
arrested.

176
00:09:04,839 --> 00:09:06,321
He denies any wrongdoing.

177
00:09:06,641 --> 00:09:09,841
June 25, Wirecard files for insolvency.

178
00:09:09,841 --> 00:09:12,641
The first company in the
DAX ever to go bust.

179
00:09:12,641 --> 00:09:16,001
The man brought in as interim chief
executive had joined the company the

180
00:09:16,001 --> 00:09:17,397
evening before he took over.

181
00:09:17,557 --> 00:09:20,037
He stayed eighty-five days.

182
00:09:20,437 --> 00:09:22,277
One person was already gone.

183
00:09:22,277 --> 00:09:26,197
Jan Marsalek, the chief operating
officer, who ran the partner business

184
00:09:26,197 --> 00:09:27,197
in Asia.

185
00:09:26,997 --> 00:09:29,139
He was fired, and he disappeared.

186
00:09:29,139 --> 00:09:33,139
He was later traced to Belarus, and
then reportedly to Moscow.

187
00:09:33,139 --> 00:09:37,699
In 2025, a court in London convicted members
of a spy ring working for Russia.

188
00:09:37,699 --> 00:09:39,914
Prosecutors said Marsalek had directed it.

189
00:09:40,073 --> 00:09:42,713
He is still wanted by German police.

190
00:09:43,914 --> 00:09:46,313
Part 5 Following the Real Money

191
00:09:46,313 --> 00:09:48,073
So, back to our question.

192
00:09:48,073 --> 00:09:51,388
Where did the one point nine
billion euros go?

193
00:09:51,868 --> 00:09:56,108
The short answer, according to the insolvency
administrator and the prosecutors,

194
00:09:56,108 --> 00:09:57,108
is nowhere.

195
00:09:57,068 --> 00:09:58,193
It was never there.

196
00:09:58,193 --> 00:10:02,593
The administrator, Michael Jaffé, found
no trace of the money and no sign

197
00:10:02,593 --> 00:10:04,964
that the partner business
ever really existed.

198
00:10:04,964 --> 00:10:09,204
Oliver Bellenhaus, who ran Wirecard's unit
in Dubai and became a key witness

199
00:10:09,204 --> 00:10:11,764
for the prosecution, put it
this way in court.

200
00:10:11,764 --> 00:10:14,244
The numbers were synthetic fictions.

201
00:10:14,797 --> 00:10:17,117
But that doesn't mean no money was lost.

202
00:10:17,117 --> 00:10:18,397
Real money was.

203
00:10:18,397 --> 00:10:21,037
It just came from somewhere else.

204
00:10:21,037 --> 00:10:23,225
Follow the cash that actually existed.

205
00:10:23,465 --> 00:10:27,706
Wirecard had borrowed about one point
seven five billion euros from a group

206
00:10:27,706 --> 00:10:29,305
of around fifteen banks.

207
00:10:29,305 --> 00:10:32,106
It had sold five hundred
million euros in bonds.

208
00:10:32,106 --> 00:10:35,110
And SoftBank had put in about
nine hundred million.

209
00:10:35,270 --> 00:10:37,750
That was real money, from real lenders.

210
00:10:37,750 --> 00:10:41,670
And according to Jaffé's testimony,
Wirecard burned through about one point

211
00:10:41,670 --> 00:10:45,561
one billion euros of it over the
years, just to keep running.

212
00:10:46,121 --> 00:10:47,481
The banks felt it.

213
00:10:47,481 --> 00:10:51,241
Commerzbank, the same bank Wirecard had
pushed out of the DAX, reportedly

214
00:10:51,241 --> 00:10:54,761
set aside about one hundred seventy-five
million euros for its share of

215
00:10:54,761 --> 00:10:55,761
the loans.

216
00:10:55,561 --> 00:10:57,191
ING, about the same.

217
00:10:57,350 --> 00:11:01,350
One bank reportedly sold its slice, about
one hundred twenty million euros,

218
00:11:01,350 --> 00:11:03,910
for around eighteen cents on the euro.

219
00:11:04,390 --> 00:11:07,706
In other words, the fake profits
weren't hiding a pile of cash.

220
00:11:07,866 --> 00:11:10,106
They were hiding a business
that was losing it.

221
00:11:10,106 --> 00:11:13,706
Prosecutors say the made-up numbers were
used to keep the banks lending.

222
00:11:13,706 --> 00:11:16,906
They put the damage at about
three billion euros.

223
00:11:17,466 --> 00:11:19,240
And then there are the shareholders.

224
00:11:19,399 --> 00:11:23,399
A company once worth twenty-four billion
euros was suddenly worth almost

225
00:11:23,399 --> 00:11:24,399
nothing.

226
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:28,266
Creditors have filed claims of about
fifteen point four billion euros.

227
00:11:28,266 --> 00:11:32,266
The insolvency estate holds roughly
six hundred fifty million.

228
00:11:32,266 --> 00:11:36,505
And in 2025, Germany's highest civil
court ruled that shareholders stand

229
00:11:36,505 --> 00:11:38,593
behind ordinary creditors in line.

230
00:11:38,832 --> 00:11:43,872
About fifty thousand shareholders who filed
claims are expected to get nothing.

231
00:11:44,192 --> 00:11:46,707
What was left of the real
business was sold off.

232
00:11:46,787 --> 00:11:50,547
The Spanish bank Santander bought
Wirecard's core technology.

233
00:11:50,547 --> 00:11:53,507
Units from Brazil to Asia
went to other buyers.

234
00:11:53,507 --> 00:11:56,947
That money goes to the
creditors, not the shareholders.

235
00:11:58,276 --> 00:12:00,596
Part 6 So, Where Did It Go?

236
00:12:00,596 --> 00:12:02,985
So let's put the money back together.

237
00:12:03,545 --> 00:12:07,545
The one point nine billion euros
never sat in the Philippines.

238
00:12:07,545 --> 00:12:11,145
It existed on paper, in contracts and
bank letters that turned out to be

239
00:12:11,145 --> 00:12:12,145
false.

240
00:12:11,987 --> 00:12:16,307
The money that really disappeared came from
lenders, bondholders and shareholders.

241
00:12:16,307 --> 00:12:18,841
Some of it paid for a company
that was losing money.

242
00:12:19,001 --> 00:12:22,921
Some of it, a Munich court found in a
civil case, went out as loans that

243
00:12:22,921 --> 00:12:24,565
should never have been approved.

244
00:12:24,565 --> 00:12:28,272
That court ordered Braun and two other
former executives to pay one hundred

245
00:12:28,272 --> 00:12:30,805
forty million euros in damages.

246
00:12:31,445 --> 00:12:32,944
What about the watchdogs?

247
00:12:33,104 --> 00:12:37,344
Germany's audit regulator fined EY five
hundred thousand euros and banned

248
00:12:37,344 --> 00:12:41,584
it from taking new audit clients among
large listed companies for two years.

249
00:12:41,584 --> 00:12:44,025
EY says it has learned from the case.

250
00:12:44,185 --> 00:12:47,945
The head of BaFin, who had defended the
regulator, later called the scandal

251
00:12:47,945 --> 00:12:52,025
a complete disaster, and left in 2021.

252
00:12:52,185 --> 00:12:54,650
And some people did make
money from the fall.

253
00:12:54,809 --> 00:12:58,569
Short sellers who had bet against the
shares, the same people Wirecard had

254
00:12:58,569 --> 00:13:01,770
called liars, profited when
the price collapsed.

255
00:13:02,249 --> 00:13:06,030
In Singapore, at least four people
have been sentenced so far.

256
00:13:06,030 --> 00:13:11,069
In January 2026, the man whose firm sent
those false letters about the escrow

257
00:13:11,069 --> 00:13:13,321
money got ten years in prison.

258
00:13:13,801 --> 00:13:18,201
In Munich, Markus Braun has been on
trial since December 2022.

259
00:13:18,282 --> 00:13:20,521
More than two hundred ninety
days of hearings.

260
00:13:20,521 --> 00:13:22,841
A verdict is expected around
the end of this year.

261
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:24,775
Braun denies all the charges.

262
00:13:24,775 --> 00:13:28,775
He says he was deceived by Marsalek and
others, and that he kept most of

263
00:13:28,775 --> 00:13:30,855
his own fortune in Wirecard shares.

264
00:13:30,855 --> 00:13:34,320
Until a court decides, he is
presumed innocent.

265
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:37,200
The missing two billion was
never in a bank.

266
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:38,560
It was in a spreadsheet.

267
00:13:38,560 --> 00:13:42,450
The money that really vanished belonged
to the people who believed it.

268
